articles.content_en:
A loan contract between natural persons may be deemed to be formed under any of the following circumstances:
(1) where payment is made in cash, when the borrower receives the loan;
(2) where payment is made by bank transfer, online electronic remittance, or other means, when the funds arrive in the borrower's account;
(3) where payment is made by negotiable instrument, when the borrower acquires the rights to the negotiable instrument according to law;
(4) where the lender authorizes the borrower to control a specific fund account, when the borrower obtains actual control over the account;
(5) where the lender provides the loan by other means agreed with the borrower and actually completes performance.

Where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall determine that the private lending contract is void:
(1) obtaining a loan from a financial institution and relending it;
(2) relending funds obtained by borrowing from other for-profit legal persons, raising funds from employees of the entity, or illegally absorbing deposits from the public;
(3) a lender who has not obtained lending qualification according to law provides loans to unspecified persons in society for profit-making purposes;
(4) the lender knew or should have known in advance that the borrower would use the loan for illegal or criminal activities and still provided the loan;
(5) violating mandatory provisions of laws and administrative regulations;
(6) violating public order and good morals.

When hearing a private lending dispute case, if a People's Court discovers any of the following circumstances, it shall strictly examine facts such as the cause, time, place, source of funds, delivery method, flow of funds, and the relationship and economic conditions of the lending and borrowing parties, and comprehensively determine whether it constitutes a false civil action:
(1) the lender obviously lacks the capacity to lend;
(2) the facts and reasons on which the lender's action is based are obviously inconsistent with common sense;
(3) the lender cannot submit debt instruments, or the submitted debt instruments may have been forged;
(4) the parties have participated in private lending actions multiple times within a certain period;
(5) a party refuses to appear in court without justifiable reasons, and the authorized agent's statements about the lending facts are unclear or contradictory;
(6) the parties have no dispute over the occurrence of the lending facts, or the claims and defenses are obviously inconsistent with common sense;
(7) the borrower's spouse or partner, or other creditors outside the case, raise objections with factual basis;
(8) the parties have transferred property at low prices in other disputes;
(9) the parties improperly waive rights;
(10) other circumstances where a false private lending action may exist.
